<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/intermediate/coasts-and-oceans">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Coasts are <strong>the busiest interface on Earth: waves, tides, rivers and humans all
    converging on a narrow strip</strong> — home to roughly 40% of humanity within 100 km of the
    sea. Coastlines are not lines at all but processes: erosion here, deposition there, always
    adjusting to waves, sediment supply and (now) rising seas.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the coastal machine, from wave energy to rising seas" widget="equationStep" config={coastCfg} />

  <h2>Ocean geography beyond the beach</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Currents are climate:</strong> the Gulf Stream warms Europe by several degrees; the global "conveyor belt" redistributes heat on a ~1,000-year circuit. Its possible slowdown (AMOC weakening) is a climate tipping point (the environmental-science ladder covers it).</li>
    <li><strong>El Niño:</strong> a Pacific-wide oscillation that flips weather worldwide — droughts in Australia, floods in Peru. Recognised first by Peruvian fishers noticing warm "El Niño" currents at Christmas.</li>
    <li><strong>Mangroves, reefs and dunes:</strong> nature's coastal defences — wave-energy absorbers, nurseries and carbon stores. Concrete sea walls cost more and last less; half the world's mangroves are already gone.</li>
    <li><strong>The deep is the default Earth:</strong> oceans cover 71% of the planet and over 80% is unmapped at modern resolution — Seabed 2030 aims to fix that. More high-resolution maps exist of Mars' surface than of the ocean floor.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Haslett, Coastal Systems; NOAA ocean data; IPCC Special Report on the Ocean (2019).</p>
</TopicPage>
